{"level":"info","ts":1738780854.680501,"logger":"genai-provider.chat","caller":"genai/gemini.go:78","msg":"Handling text","model":"models/gemini-2.0-flash-exp","id":"f7782bd0-b07e-4778-a2f1-5727038016ca","content":"Okay, I understand the issue. Here's the plan to address it:\n\n1. **Explore the repository:** I'll start by listing the files in the repository to understand the project structure and identify potential files to modify or create.\n2. **Examine relevant files:** Based on the file structure, I'll read the contents of files that seem related to pull request handling, event processing, or label management. This will help me understand the existing codebase and how to integrate the new functionality.\n3. **Implement the tracking logic:** I'll need to implement logic to:\n    - Listen for pull request events (creation, updates, merges, closes).\n    - Check if the `dev-team` label is present.\n    - Maintain a list of tracked pull requests (likely in a file or data structure).\n    - Remove pull requests from the tracking list when they are closed, merged, or the label is removed.\n    - Listen for comments on tracked pull requests.\n    - Add a new commit to the pull request addressing the comment.\n4. **Write the code:** I'll write the code to implement the tracking logic, making sure to handle different scenarios and edge cases.\n5. **Test the changes:** I'll add some test cases to ensure that the tracking mechanism works correctly and that pull requests are added and removed from the tracking list as expected.\n6. **Write the changes to the file:** I'll use the writeFile tool to write the code to the appropriate file(s).\n\nLet's start by listing the files in the repository.\n\n"}
