{"level":"info","ts":1738806547.2362957,"logger":"genai-provider.chat","caller":"genai/gemini.go:78","msg":"Handling text","model":"models/gemini-2.0-flash","id":"dc3c6018-7dad-400f-9beb-fd2c78f37eef","content":"The `go.mod` file looks correct. The module path is `github.com/jbutlerdev/dev-team`, which matches the import paths used in the code.\n\nSince the module path seems correct, the issue might be related to how the code is being built or run. It's possible that the Go toolchain is not correctly configured to resolve the local import paths.\n\nUnfortunately, I don't have enough information to diagnose this further. I don't have access to the build environment or the specific commands being used to build and run the code.\n\nHowever, here are some suggestions that might help:\n\n*   **Verify the `GOPATH` and `GO111MODULE` environment variables:** Make sure these variables are correctly configured. `GO111MODULE` should be set to `on` to enable module support.\n*   **Try running `go mod tidy`:** This command can help resolve any inconsistencies in the `go.mod` file and download any missing dependencies.\n*   **Check the build commands:** Ensure that the build commands are correctly referencing the module path.\n*   **Verify the directory structure:** Double-check that the directory structure matches the module path.\n*   **Clean the build cache:** Try cleaning the build cache to remove any stale build artifacts.\n\nI apologize that I cannot provide a more specific solution. Without access to the build environment, it's difficult to diagnose the root cause of the import error.\n"}
